00:00:06.687 --> 00:00:08.020
EDMOND CHAN: Hello, and welcome.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.790
I'm Edmond from Square,
and with me are Lak

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.360
and Yufeng from Google.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.220
We're going to be
talking to you today

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.970
about useful patterns for
developing, operationalizing,

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.980
and maintaining machine
learning models.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.780
Machine learning
involves a few steps.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.030
From organizing your
data, you gather it,

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:29.380
engineer your features,
create and train the model,

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.790
and you finally deploy it.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:35.200
But it's actually iterative.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.370
You need to-- there we go.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.080
I didn't know there's
an animation there.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.030
It's actually quite iterative.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.410
You need to first organize
your data-- sorry.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.420
You're not done once
you've trained and deployed

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.260
your model.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.990
You need to continuously
monitor its performance,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:00.510
add features,
retrain periodically.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:04.200
It's a team effort involving
data analysts, data engineers,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.970
and data scientists.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.175
Typically, your
subject matter experts,

00:01:08.175 --> 00:01:12.250
your data engineers
will gather all the data

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.330
with inputs from
data scientists.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.850
Your data scientists
will join all the data,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.750
create the features,
create and train the model.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.540
And data engineers will be
responsible for maintaining

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.880
the training prediction
infrastructure.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.350
And everyone will monitor
model performance, all

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.930
the different aspects of it.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:33.060
Data analysts will be
focused on the outputs

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.260
of the model, their
distributions, how actionable

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.170
they are, whether they're
enabling good decisions or not.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.220
Data scientists will be more
focused on the distributions

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.690
of the inputs themselves.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.400
Any shifts in those
might represent

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.280
a problem with your
data ingestion pipeline,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.620
or maybe a shift
in the population.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.230
And the data engineers
will be focusing

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:58.430
on uptime and resilience.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.670
And thus, because we have so
many people working on so many

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.910
moving parts simultaneously,
operationalizing

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.040
ML models is actually
quite difficult.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.905
And now Lak is going to discuss
some of those challenges.

00:02:13.249 --> 00:02:14.790
VALLIAPPA LAKSHMANAN:
Thanks, Edmond.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.770
Yeah, so operationalizing
an ML model,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:22.170
by which we mean going from a
trained model to a model that's

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.860
deployed in production that
you can use to make predictions

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.510
with, that's hard.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.950
And that's the
chasm in our title.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.930
There's this big chasm
between training the model

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.610
and getting it into production.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.090
So let's talk
about, number one--

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.600
or number zero-- why is it so
hard to put a machine learning

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.850
model into production?

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:45.690
What makes it hard?

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.130
I mean, it's just software.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.140
We know how to deploy software.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.170
So I mean, what is so
special about machine

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.450
learning models that
it's so hard to put them

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.750
into production?

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:02.400
So first thing is this whole
concept of reproducibility.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.530
The thing is, when
you train your model,

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.720
what are you training on?

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.730
You're essentially training
on historical data, data that

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.220
now is in a data warehouse.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.590
You basically are taking that
data, it's in a data warehouse,

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.260
and then you're training
your model on it.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.620
But when you come
to predictions,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.714
what are you predicting on?

00:03:24.714 --> 00:03:26.130
You're not making
your predictions

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.960
on data in the data warehouse.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.610
That's historical data.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:33.490
What you care about is
predictions in a real time

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:34.350
stream.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.930
You're making predictions
on data that's coming in.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.740
So it may be software,
but these two

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.960
are different software systems.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.430
You have one software
system that's

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:49.040
about historical data and
data preservation and so on.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.940
And you have another
software system

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.690
that's about real time,
that's about getting things

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.870
very quickly, that's
about user interactivity.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.400
It's about speed.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.040
So they're very, very
different kinds of systems.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.210
And everything that you
do in the training step

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.990
has to be repeated in
the deployment step.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.840
And that reproducibility
is very hard.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.190
So if we look at
patterns that help

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.000
us get that reproducibility
so that the steps that you did

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.280
during training,
you can basically

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.700
repeat them or reproduce them
in your deployment system.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.080
The second thing is that
everything that you do

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.950
needs to be portable
between the training system

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.520
and the deployment system.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.380
And unfortunately,
these two things--

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.420
the hardware and the software--

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.250
are often very different.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.960
You might train on the cloud,
but you might do an inference

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:49.540
on a phone.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.950
It may be the same software.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:57.450
But the software that runs on
the cloud, obviously the system

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.430
is very different
than something that's

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.060
going to run on the phone.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.830
And you need everything that
you do, all your ML models,

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.610
all your ML systems, to be
portable between the hardware

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.610
and the software that's actually
different between the training

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:12.840
and inference systems.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.900
So we look at portability
patterns, ways

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.870
to make sure that
things remain portable.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.860
The third thing is that
whatever platform that you use,

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.720
it needs to be flexible.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.000
And the reason it
needs to be flexible

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.660
is that your skill sets
on the training side

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.600
and the priorities
on the training side

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.860
are very different from the
skill sets and priorities

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.750
on the inference side.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.290
On the training side,
who's doing the training?

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.030
It's going to be
your data people.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.860
And what do they care about?

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.480
Data, number one.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.600
But they also care about
things like accuracy.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.990
Can I train on all the data?

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.706
They care about scale.

00:05:55.706 --> 00:05:57.330
But when they talk
about scale, they're

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.730
talking about scale
in terms of, am I

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.830
able to scale to all my data?

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.320
They're talking about research.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.780
They're talking about being
able to use better models,

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.990
to be able to use the
most advanced models that

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.040
are available.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.950
And they're also talking
about the models themselves,

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.810
being able to basically
accommodate them.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.390
So that's on the training side.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.780
So their priorities are
in terms of accuracy,

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.480
in terms of scale, in terms
of being able to train

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:25.590
on all their data.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:30.360
And their skill sets tend
to be things like statistics

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.440
and Python and so on.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.990
Now, look on the other side.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.160
On the inference side,
who's doing the inference?

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:38.790
It's your engineering team.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.060
What do they care about?

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.500
Resilience.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.630
They don't want
the thing to crash.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.850
They don't want the
pager to go off.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.470
That's the number one priority.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:51.780
The priority is very different.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.130
They also care about scale.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.870
But the scale that an
engineer cares about

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:00.950
is different from the scale
that your data scientist

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:01.820
cares about.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:05.090
Your data scientist wants
to train on all the data.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.240
The engineer wants to
make sure that they

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.870
can handle all million users
that are going to hit your web

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.300
page at lunchtime.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.340
The scale that they're talking
about is very different.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.310
They care about the
engineering quality.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.470
They care about maintainability.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:22.820
They care about speed.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.070
They care about applications.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:28.080
So the priorities and
skill sets are different.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.770
And so if you pick a
machine learning platform,

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:35.060
any platform you pick has to
be flexible to accommodate

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:39.020
both of these types of users.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:43.990
And finally, you need to
basically deal with agility.

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:48.500
And as kind of a joke, but
you kind of train your model

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:49.990
on historical data.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:53.110
You train your model on things
that look like dinosaurs.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.180
And then you start
running your model,

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.510
and they basically
are dealt with birds.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.900
Your data has drifted,
has evolved over time,

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:05.060
and you basically get something
that is kind of the same thing,

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:06.640
but not quite the same thing.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.187
And you need that agility to
deal with this model drift

00:08:10.187 --> 00:08:11.020
because, guess what?

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.400
The real world, things
change over time.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.240
So the thing that you
trained on and the thing

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.870
that you're predicting on, there
is going to be model drift.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.450
And the question is,
how do you recognize it?

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.100
How do you accommodate for it?

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:25.390
How do you fix it?

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:30.460
So we look at patterns
that deal with agility.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.809
So first to talk about
reproducibility patterns.

00:08:34.809 --> 00:08:37.419
So first one, so what
are some of the ways

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:40.240
that we basically
get reproducibility?

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.870
So first thing is
what we talked about.

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:45.820
You train on historical data.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:50.570
You predict on real-time
streaming data.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.090
So how do you deal with that?

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:56.320
You want to basically reuse your
pipeline, your data pipeline,

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.540
your data preprocessing, as much
as possible between your batch

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:06.850
historical system and your
streaming real-time system.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:11.440
To date, there is
still only one system,

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.980
one API that allows
you to do data

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:20.050
processing with the same code
on both batch and stream.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:21.700
And that's Beam, Apache Beam.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.810
In fact, the B-E--

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.530
B is for batch, and
E-A-M is for stream.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:29.500
So it's about a single,
consistent thing

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.930
that lets you deal
with batch and stream

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.600
so that you can basically
have the same code.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.970
And in our case,
we run Apache Beam

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:38.770
on Google Cloud and Dataflow.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.930
That's why you see Dataflow
in the diagram there.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.850
But the code that you
write is open source.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:43.600
It's Apache Beam.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.210
So pattern number
one, Apache Beam

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:51.080
to reuse this pipeline code
between training and inference.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:55.480
So the reason that we do
this is because all the data

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.880
transformations that you need
to do on the training dataset,

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.560
they have to get applied
during inference, too.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.320
So you take your raw data.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:06.430
You preprocess it.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:07.090
You clean it.

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:08.590
You create features.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:10.000
And it is these
features that you

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.980
give to your training model.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.465
And now your
training model needs

00:10:13.465 --> 00:10:16.000
to basically do predictions.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.510
And so you take that trained
model and you deploy it.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:24.310
But to that deployed web
application, what comes about

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.580
is essentially the raw
inputs that the clients need.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:31.000
It's exactly the same
data that they need.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.360
The question now is, how are we
going to do this pre-processing

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:36.940
and feature creation
so that reproducibility

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.410
is super important?

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.860
How do we reproduce everything
that we did in our training

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:44.860
system on the inference side?

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.530
So there are a couple
of ways to do it.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:51.340
If, for example, you do
preprocessing in TensorFlow,

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:54.400
you can do this reproduction.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:55.900
So you have your
preprocessing code.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.480
You have your feature
creation code.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.550
And then you create
your preprocess features

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.440
and you train your
model on them.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:04.060
It is very efficient.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:07.270
And if you are using
the Estimator API,

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:10.540
you basically have something
that is called feature columns.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:13.150
And everything that you
do in a feature column

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.890
will be remembered in
the TensorFlow graph.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:18.350
So when you checkpoint
a TensorFlow model,

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:22.120
all of those preprocessing
steps are saved in the graph

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:26.500
so that when you then go out to
go predict it, it's automatic.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:29.230
All of those steps
that you did get

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.560
carried out because you
used feature columns.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.870
So that is great.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:37.660
But there are a couple
of caveats here.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:41.500
The first caveat is that
it's only things that you

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:43.000
do in a feature column.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.940
It's only things that you
use TensorFlow methods with.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:49.120
What if you did
preprocessing that doesn't

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.540
happen in the feature column?

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.100
Now you have to deal
with it some other way.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:57.690
The second thing is, and
this is a very general,

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:02.340
high-level restriction,
your TensorFlow model, when

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.900
you train it, what does it see?

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:08.140
It essentially sees
a batch of examples.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:11.570
But in that sense, it only
sees one example at a time.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:17.830
It doesn't actually
see examples in order.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:22.600
So any of the preprocessing
that you do on that one training

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.310
example is fine.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:30.380
But if your preprocessing
involves a moving average,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.190
will you ever have a
moving average as an input?

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.510
Sure, right?

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:37.270
If you ever have a
moving average, that

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:41.170
moving average, the way you
compute the moving average,

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:44.300
there's nothing you can do
inside the TensorFlow feature

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:45.760
transform to do that.

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:48.430
It doesn't work because
the TensorFlow model only

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:50.530
sees that one example.

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.600
It doesn't know how you
computed that feature

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.620
in the first place.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.230
So there is that second issue.

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.410
So it's efficient,
but it's restricted

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:01.690
to TensorFlow methods
in a feature column,

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:04.600
and it's only that one input.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:06.280
The other approach
that you can do

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:09.400
is that you can do this
thing in Apache Beam.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.110
So that's what I
just showed you.

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.610
You do it in that Beam pipeline.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.580
You do your actual
preprocessing,

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:19.090
you compute your moving
average in Apache Beam.

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:20.190
So that's great.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:21.320
It has state.

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:24.370
You compute it in Python,
you compute it in Java.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:26.560
And you compute that
feature and you're training

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:28.210
the model on the feature.

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:30.160
Then when you're
doing the predictions,

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:34.690
you run the exact same pipeline
to create the exact same moving

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:37.870
average and you give
it to your model.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:42.310
So this way now it
sees a full dataset.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:46.300
It scales really well
because Beam is distributed.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:50.020
But it cannot take
advantage of accelerators.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.520
What do you mean
by accelerators?

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.250
GPUs, TPUs, et cetera.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:58.890
This is OK if it's
a moving average.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:03.680
It's not OK if you're
basically running

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:06.870
the first half of
the inception model,

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:10.040
so basically create an embedding
that you're basically using

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:11.480
as an input to your model.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:15.870
Now creating that
feature is so expensive.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:19.650
You can't really do that
in something like Beam.

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:25.560
So how do we basically
do reproducibility

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:29.440
that can accommodate for
both of these things?

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:33.510
And that's what
tf.transform does.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.300
So TensorFlow
transform is a library

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:42.860
that is a hybrid of Apache
Beam and TensorFlow.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:44.480
How is it a hybrid?

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:50.900
You do your analysis during
training in Apache Beam.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:52.940
So you can look at
all your dataset.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.140
You can compute your
moving averages.

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:56.870
You can find your
mins and maxes.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:58.760
You can find your unique values.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:02.130
You can do all of
those things in Beam.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:07.620
And then at prediction
time in TensorFlow

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:10.930
you can actually apply
those transformations.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:14.010
So you get the scale
of Beam and you get

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:17.560
the efficiency of TensorFlow.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:21.520
And everything is
remembered from what

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:23.620
you did during
training to what you

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:27.100
need to do during prediction.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:31.330
So pattern number one
for reproducibility,

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.270
TensorFlow transform.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:37.070
So you basically get, as I said,
the efficiency of TensorFlow

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:40.140
during inference and the
scalability of the Beam

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:40.970
during training.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:42.700
So it's not just Beam.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:44.260
It's not just TensorFlow.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.210
It's that right
combination of the two.

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.380
The second thing that
you want to think

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.150
about when it comes
to reproducibility

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:58.120
gets back into the development
of the model itself.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.190
And this is where you want to
make sure that you are never

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:06.540
copying data because every
time you make a copy of data,

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.904
you run this risk that the
thing that you train on

00:16:09.904 --> 00:16:11.820
and the thing that you're
going to predict on,

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:13.194
there have been
other things that

00:16:13.194 --> 00:16:14.470
have happened in the middle.

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:16.890
So one of the things
that we always suggest

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:18.990
is keep your data in BigQuery.

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:21.000
You can share the data set.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.670
You can share your
queries in BigQuery.

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:26.630
You can actually save your
data on cloud storage.

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:28.830
And you can run a
federated query.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:31.740
So you can run your SQL query
on the data that exists already

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:35.670
in Cloud Storage or in Cloud
Bigtable or on Google Drive.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:38.910
But the point being, don't
make copies of your data.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:40.620
Don't move your data around.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:42.300
Keep your data in place.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:43.770
Join it in place.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.390
Share the data sets.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.940
Share the queries so
that different people can

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:50.710
run different analytics on it.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.200
So join your data in place.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:53.340
Don't move it around.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:56.280
So if you have structured data,
if you have streaming data,

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:57.820
use BigQuery.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:00.550
If you have archival
data, unstructured data,

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:01.980
use Cloud Storage.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:05.640
If you have transactional
data, time series data,

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.369
internet of things data,
use Cloud Bigtable.

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:11.490
If you have business types of
data, spreadsheets, et cetera,

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:13.020
use Google Drive.

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:15.930
And the cool thing is
that all of these things,

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.329
you can run a
federated query on.

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:21.030
So you can keep
your data in place

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.190
and don't make copies of it.

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:25.380
And by not making
copies of it, you

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:29.800
can limit your
reproducibility problems.

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:32.980
And then you can
access that same data

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:34.787
with different kinds of tools.

00:17:34.787 --> 00:17:37.120
So as we just talked about,
you can do federated queries

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:38.110
with BigQuery.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:39.770
But you can use
Dataflow and Apache

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:43.130
Beam so you can do your
batch and stream processing.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.430
So you explore your
data in BigQuery.

00:17:45.430 --> 00:17:48.610
And then you train and
you predict with Dataflow.

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:50.560
You haven't changed the data.

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:51.940
You haven't moved the data.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.901
So you don't have to worry that
this data that you explored

00:17:54.901 --> 00:17:56.650
and this data that
you're training, again,

00:17:56.650 --> 00:17:57.980
are actually different.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:00.750
So you can do Dataproc,
Hadoop and Spark workloads

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:02.470
in machine learning, et cetera.

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:04.600
And this allows you to
democratize your data

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:06.070
in sites across your org.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.870
You can save and
share your queries.

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:09.880
You can create good
reports with BigQuery.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:11.530
You can share them as Sheets.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:15.400
You can use partner tools like
Tableau, Looker, et cetera,

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:16.330
to share this data.

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:20.350
But the key principle here
is, keep the data in place.

00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:23.290
Do your analytics
with multiple tools,

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.210
but don't move your data around.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.850
And to talk about how
Square does this, Edmond.

00:18:32.475 --> 00:18:34.600
EDMOND CHAN: So the rules
governing how much Square

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:36.060
should pay its
financial partners

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:40.170
for processing any given
payment are extremely Byzantine.

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.270
They're scattered across
multiple rulebooks,

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:46.070
with each card, network, and
currency having its own book.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:48.286
Some of these books are
over 1,000 page long.

00:18:48.286 --> 00:18:50.535
And they change twice a year,
every April and October.

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:59.610
So it's actually
quite difficult for us

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:02.932
to know how much we should be
paying for any one payment.

00:19:02.932 --> 00:19:04.890
It's far too difficult
to generate and maintain

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:05.515
a rules engine.

00:19:05.515 --> 00:19:07.765
So what we've done is we've
created a machine learning

00:19:07.765 --> 00:19:10.740
model to verify our cost for
us, the transaction cost anomaly

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.120
detector, or TCAD.

00:19:15.632 --> 00:19:16.620
Oh, sorry.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:20.440
Through TCAD, we've
discovered over $2 million

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:23.020
worth of transaction anomalies.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:26.320
Much of it was recoverable
because they were bona fide

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:29.240
transaction cost errors.

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:32.990
And a large part of achieving
that result has been limiting

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:34.700
training-serving
skew, which we've

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:39.200
done by using the same queries,
using the same BigQuery tables,

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:42.050
have training and inference,
therefore both times we're

00:19:42.050 --> 00:19:43.614
using the same data.

00:19:43.614 --> 00:19:45.530
We've also performed all
of our pre-processing

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:49.700
within TensorFlow using the
TF feature column module.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:56.390
And this ensures that at
training and inference,

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:58.640
we're using the
same vocabularies

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.220
for categorical features,
the same scaling

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:03.710
for our numeric features.

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:05.390
This has worked
quite well for us.

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:09.140
But it does require us to first
define these vocabularies,

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:12.805
define these numeric ranges
and encode them in our model.

00:20:12.805 --> 00:20:14.430
In future iterations,
we're most likely

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.550
going to be moving towards
TensorFlow transform

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:17.924
to further simplify
this process.

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:22.940
Now, another pattern that
we've been using in TCAD

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.060
is portability.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:27.080
And to see that, let's take a
look at the TCAD architecture

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:29.160
itself.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:32.840
Now, let's start at the
top left with the training.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:36.312
We have our historical cost
data stored in BigQuery.

00:20:36.312 --> 00:20:38.270
And we're training with
that data on ML Engine.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:43.710
And then when it comes
time for inference,

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:46.210
which is the lower
half of this diagram,

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:50.040
we have our trained model
stored in Cloud Storage.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:53.340
And we have a data pipeline,
a Cloud Dataflow pipeline,

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:56.550
which also takes our historical
cost data from BigQuery,

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:01.380
the same sources training,
and computes or estimates

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:04.170
what our true costs
should be, compares

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:08.820
that against our actual cost,
computes aggregates, passes

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:11.970
them all along to Stackdriver,
where we have dashboards

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:14.760
and alerts, and, if necessary,
additional human review

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:17.425
and further action.

00:21:17.425 --> 00:21:19.050
This has all been
working great for us.

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:20.349
But we didn't start with that.

00:21:20.349 --> 00:21:22.890
We have to go from an on-premise
solution to a complete cloud

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:26.640
solution where we moved
all our data to BigQuery.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:29.550
We moved our storage to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:32.160
And we moved our inference
and data processing pipeline

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:33.600
to Dataflow.

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:36.000
But all along the way,
we were able to retain

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:37.230
the same TensorFlow model.

00:21:41.099 --> 00:21:42.640
VALLIAPPA LAKSHMANAN:
Thanks, Edmond.

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:46.720
So as Edmond said, the key
idea is this whole idea

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:50.420
of portability, that they were
able to make all these changes,

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:52.030
but at the bottom
of it, they were

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.190
able to keep the exact
same TensorFlow model.

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:56.650
And that's one thing that I
think you keep hearing over

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:58.870
and over again, is
this idea of being

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:03.610
able to do porting from on-prem
to cloud, from your cloud

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:05.120
to your device, et cetera.

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:10.450
And TensorFlow is a big part
of providing that portability.

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:13.260
So why do you need portability?

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:15.520
When we designed
TensorFlow, this whole idea

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:18.430
was that we needed to
support different training

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:20.090
and inference environments.

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:22.540
So when you talk about
where do you train,

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:24.460
you might train on the cloud.

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:27.640
You might train on
a Kubernetes system.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:31.780
You might train with
programs written in Python.

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:35.030
TensorFlow is all
Python, primarily.

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:38.020
The training code might
need accelerators,

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.890
like GPUs or your
custom ASIC chips,

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:43.870
like Tensor processing units.

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:46.450
So you need that
portability that happens.

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:48.880
The fact that training
happens on all these kinds

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:52.720
of environments, then you go
over to the inference side.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:54.160
Where do people do inference?

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.720
Where do they run predictions?

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:58.500
Well, they may run
predictions in Kubernetes.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:01.150
They may run it on GKE
with Kubernetes Engine.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:03.390
They may run it as part
of a web application.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:05.680
They may run it
in mobile devices.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:07.420
They might run it on JavaScript.

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:09.253
How many of you have
you heard of TensorFlow

00:23:09.253 --> 00:23:11.240
on JavaScript, TensorFlow.js?

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:13.120
That's part of the
portability idea.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.080
You train the
model on the cloud,

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:17.680
but you run it inside
your web browser.

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:20.350
You run it with Java.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:24.160
The fact that you trained it,
created your model with Python,

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:28.900
doesn't mean that you have
to run it in a Python system.

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:33.370
That's a very key, important
design goal behind TensorFlow

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:36.010
is to support all these
different training

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.350
and inference environments.

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:41.650
So the pattern here
is use TensorFlow.

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:45.010
TensorFlow is what gives
you that portability.

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:47.950
And in terms of
these environments,

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:49.990
I like to think of it
as three different types

00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:51.370
of environments.

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:54.880
You have the environments
that are all about simplicity.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:57.670
You have a fully
managed Cloud ML engine.

00:23:57.670 --> 00:23:58.630
Everything is there.

00:23:58.630 --> 00:24:00.330
You don't have to
spin up a cluster.

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.410
You just submit
your TensorFlow code

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:04.060
and you have your model trained.

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:06.760
Or if you want to do
development of a model

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:12.490
and you just want a VM
with TensorFlow and Jupyter

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:16.000
and everything that you would
typically need in data science,

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:19.620
you want all of that
directly installed,

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.110
there is a deep learning image.

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:23.500
You can go to the
Cloud Launcher page,

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:27.100
launch a deep-learning
image, and it basically

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:30.140
has different versions of
TensorFlow, different GPUs

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:33.340
that you can attach to it, and
you can basically get going.

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:35.350
So that's the
simplicity end of it.

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:37.660
On the fast side
of things, you want

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:39.310
to do things very,
very, very fast.

00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:40.870
You want to train very fast.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:43.920
You know the cloud GPUs,
you have GPUs on the cloud.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:46.630
But in between, you
might need portability.

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:47.980
You might need things.

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:50.980
You might need to run
inference not on the cloud.

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:53.190
And that's where
Kubeflow comes in.

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:56.650
Kubeflow is TensorFlow in
Kubernetes, an open source

00:24:56.650 --> 00:24:58.420
project led by Google.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.750
And then you have TensorFlow,
which runs in Python and C++

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.630
and JavaScript and
Java, TensorFlow Lite,

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:07.190
which runs on mobile devices.

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:10.280
So you have all of
these different choices.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:13.380
And third, to talk about
flexibility, Yufeng.

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:20.190
YUFENG GUO: OK, so we've talked
about our first two points.

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:24.480
Flexibility comes in kind of
almost when you reverse around.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:27.150
Instead of saying,
for the same model,

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:29.970
if I switch the world around
the model, what happens?

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:32.730
There's also the consideration
of when it comes time

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.250
to change the model,
how can we do that

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:39.270
without then altering the
world around that model

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.040
significantly?

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:43.520
So when it comes
to model building,

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:47.470
there's a very wide
library of choices now.

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:49.350
There's lots of
different libraries

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:52.560
you can use to build your model.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:53.920
You can use scikit-learn.

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:57.510
You can use XGBoost, Keras,
TensorFlow, and many others.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:25:59.174
There's also the TensorFlow Hub.

00:25:59.174 --> 00:26:00.840
If you haven't seen
that, I would really

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:02.006
encourage checking that out.

00:26:02.006 --> 00:26:02.680
TF Hub.

00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:06.950
It's a really neat place
to see the different models

00:26:06.950 --> 00:26:07.950
that folks are building.

00:26:07.950 --> 00:26:10.470
The hope is that
you will not only

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:13.410
be able to get value out
TF Hub to get models that

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:15.280
are out in the community,
research models,

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:18.000
things like that, but also
then be able to contribute back

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:20.470
to the community things
that you have found,

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:22.890
the new models that
you've developed.

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:26.400
And so whether you're
building just your standard,

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:28.980
fully-connected networks
or convolution networks

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:31.890
or more sophisticated things
like recurrent nets and things

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:35.490
like that, having
flexibility in what model

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:39.510
you use-- because sometimes
you might start with one,

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:44.460
do your initial analysis, and
then for the same type of data,

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:47.220
change fundamentally the
kind of model you're using.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:50.850
So that means you want to think
about what kind of interface

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:55.152
your model is providing to
your downstream consumers,

00:26:55.152 --> 00:26:56.860
such that you're able
to swap things out,

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:59.460
just general good software
engineering practices when

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:01.530
it comes to layers of
abstraction and things

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:03.590
like that.

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:05.620
And the other thing
I wanted to note

00:27:05.620 --> 00:27:08.950
is that Keras is now
also part of TensorFlow.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:10.690
So you can call tf.keras.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:13.750
I think it was 1.8 and
above that supports that.

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:16.120
And that there's
interesting ways

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:19.720
now you can combine model
building using Keras.

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:25.346
So Keras has a really great,
simple model-building API

00:27:25.346 --> 00:27:26.470
that is really easy to use.

00:27:26.470 --> 00:27:30.820
And you can combine that
with TensorFlow's execution

00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:33.040
speed and estimator frameworks.

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.790
You can stick the Keras
model inside an estimator

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:38.500
and then run that, which gives
you access to distributed

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:43.300
training, accelerators, all
without having to rework

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:46.360
the wiring of your model.

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:49.280
And so that brings us to
kind of the next point.

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:52.070
Where do you hook up the model
to, and the considerations

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:56.870
around having flexibility in
the consumption of your data.

00:27:56.870 --> 00:27:59.240
There's really highly
structured data.

00:27:59.240 --> 00:28:01.880
It may come from a variety
of different sources.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:04.940
And as you experiment with
the type and amount of data

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:07.430
you feed your model
during this training

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:10.180
process, this
iterative process, you

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.740
might find that you either
need to add more data,

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:16.820
change from one dataset to
another, or add something in,

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:19.384
and that it may go from
fitting into memory

00:28:19.384 --> 00:28:21.800
and being really easy to do
on a laptop to being something

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:23.330
significantly larger.

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:25.670
Or maybe you go from
having unstructured

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:28.606
data that's in a data lake and
you move to structured data.

00:28:28.606 --> 00:28:30.980
And then you have to think
about how you're going to port

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:32.870
that data over and
connect that in.

00:28:32.870 --> 00:28:36.530
So having flexibility in
how your model consumes

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:38.990
the different types of
data that it may encounter

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:42.200
through its lifetime is an
important design decision

00:28:42.200 --> 00:28:44.900
as you go about
kind of this task--

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:48.500
swapping out models,
swapping out datas.

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:53.340
And thirdly, the notion of
being able to iterate quickly.

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:57.749
So this is kind of a specific
note for more complex models.

00:28:57.749 --> 00:28:59.540
And when I say complex
models, I don't just

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.830
mean models that are, in and
of themselves, "sophisticated",

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:05.660
quote unquote, but
also situations

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:07.820
where you have a
requirement where

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:10.460
you need to train a
large amount of data

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:13.640
and run that through the model
during training before you can

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:17.540
get a meaningful outcome, or to
even know if your training is

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:19.320
going in the right direction.

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:23.060
And so for that, you want to
be able to train and iterate

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.587
with a reasonable
amount of speed.

00:29:25.587 --> 00:29:27.920
And there's a couple of ways
that you can achieve speed.

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:31.400
If you say, well, training
iterations are fast for me

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:35.060
because each time I do
a new training cycle,

00:29:35.060 --> 00:29:36.870
it only takes 30 seconds.

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:37.460
Great.

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:38.600
You're all set.

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:41.270
And so you can just try
something, try something,

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:43.260
try something in series.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:45.826
But what if each time you do
a training cycle, after you've

00:29:45.826 --> 00:29:47.450
already maxed out
all your options when

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:50.030
it comes to compute
resources, it still

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:55.160
takes 30 minutes or 30 hours
to do one training iteration?

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:58.370
That's when it comes time
to think about other ways.

00:29:58.370 --> 00:30:02.030
You have to think outside of
just doing one attempt and then

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:03.770
a different attempt
and moving on.

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.950
And so being able to kick
off multiple training

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:09.740
jobs in parallel
that are isolated

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:12.380
from each other, such
that you can experiment

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:17.840
across a spectrum of different
models, different parameters,

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:21.290
and potentially even different
features, different input

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:24.600
datas that go into your model.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:27.200
And so one kind of
plug I'll make here

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:31.610
is that Cloud ML Engine
supports these various facets

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:35.660
of quick iteration when it comes
to larger data sets and bigger

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:36.620
training jobs.

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:39.309
You can train multiple jobs.

00:30:39.309 --> 00:30:40.100
You kick off a job.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:41.690
You configure it
with your model.

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:42.620
You feed it your data.

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:43.740
And it's off to the races.

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:46.280
And then you can
set up another one.

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:47.600
You kick off another job.

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:50.240
And you can choose to use
the same data set or maybe

00:30:50.240 --> 00:30:53.480
a different data set, same model
or different model, et cetera.

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:56.030
And you can kick off
multiple training tasks.

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:59.420
But then with each
training task itself, you

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:04.430
can also configure
different hyperparameters.

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:09.530
So there is built into ML Engine
a hyperparameter tuning service

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:12.860
called HyperTune, fittingly.

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:16.340
And it will basically try
out different variants

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:18.980
of the same model
and try to help you,

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:22.010
guide you in which
set of hyperparameters

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:23.309
would be most appropriate.

00:31:23.309 --> 00:31:25.100
And it will basically
do these in parallel.

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:26.720
So you're kicking
off parallel jobs.

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:30.530
Each parallel job is kicking
off parallel jobs in itself.

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:33.860
So you're getting this
incredible scaling effect

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:36.320
and making yourself super,
super productive, which

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:38.270
means you can just
go make a sandwich

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:41.900
or something at that point.

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:44.550
OK, so the other
piece of flexibility I

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:46.800
want to point out-- when we
talk about model building,

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:48.640
now we flip over to deployment.

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:51.010
When we're making
predictions, making inference,

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:53.850
there's a couple of
different options and things

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:55.270
you want to keep in mind.

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:57.630
One is model stacking.

00:31:57.630 --> 00:31:59.970
Once your model is deployed,
it may be in a situation

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:03.120
where you actually have the
outputs of one model feeding

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:05.280
in as inputs to a second model.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.850
And maybe that model's outputs
feed into a third model.

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:11.100
And at first you
might look at this

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:12.684
and say, ah, that's
too sophisticated.

00:32:12.684 --> 00:32:14.933
I'm not going to have three
models feeding each other.

00:32:14.933 --> 00:32:16.050
That's silly.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:19.770
But a lot of times the things
that we do in the preprocessing

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:23.430
step can actually be
thought of in this context.

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:25.950
What is the first thing
that happens to your data

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:27.831
when you make some of
these transformations?

00:32:27.831 --> 00:32:29.580
Depending on what they
are, if they're not

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:32.491
something super trivial,
like the time series

00:32:32.491 --> 00:32:33.990
averaging, if you're
doing something

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:36.750
a little more
sophisticated, than you

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:38.790
could think of it as
you are kind of modeling

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:40.890
your data in some way.

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:42.480
And that might change.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:47.880
And you're going to need that to
show up in deployment, as well.

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:51.050
And so when you have that
situation, the interface

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:53.180
between your
different models, they

00:32:53.180 --> 00:32:55.670
need to not
encounter bottlenecks

00:32:55.670 --> 00:32:58.250
because at deployment
time, any bottleneck starts

00:32:58.250 --> 00:33:02.510
really accruing and your latency
starts building up like crazy.

00:33:02.510 --> 00:33:06.440
And then the second point
is swapping out models.

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:09.110
Your data science team goes
and they develop a model.

00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:12.290
Here I'm calling it model four
because it's the next number.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:17.000
And then a couple of weeks
later, they're like, hey,

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:18.860
we found some new data.

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:20.390
It looks kind of
different and we

00:33:20.390 --> 00:33:22.556
decided to try a
totally different model.

00:33:22.556 --> 00:33:23.930
The interface is
kind of similar,

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:25.160
but it's a different model.

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:26.076
It's really different.

00:33:26.076 --> 00:33:29.000
And so we need to
swap that out live.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.630
Can we do that?

00:33:30.630 --> 00:33:34.070
So what happens when we want
to swap in model five like that

00:33:34.070 --> 00:33:36.399
and still have
everything keep working?

00:33:36.399 --> 00:33:37.940
And so if you're in
a situation where

00:33:37.940 --> 00:33:40.070
you should be able
to do that, that's

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:42.050
something you have
to engineer upfront

00:33:42.050 --> 00:33:44.660
because if you don't
and you need to do that,

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:47.720
you're going to be in a hole.

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:51.280
And thirdly, scaling
the inference.

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:52.820
Support predictions.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:55.730
When you have lots
and lots of requests,

00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:58.890
standing up a production
server is no easy feat.

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:01.460
But standing up
multiple really quickly

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:03.710
because lots of
requests are coming in

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:05.840
is arguably even harder.

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:08.060
And so the other
side of ML Engine--

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:10.830
aside from the training and the
GPUs and the TPUs and all that

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:11.780
awesome stuff--

00:34:11.780 --> 00:34:14.190
is that you can take and
export a TensorFlow model,

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:17.710
and just give it a name and
point to it and say, here.

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:21.949
ML Engine, take that
model and deploy it for me

00:34:21.949 --> 00:34:23.870
and autoscale that for me.

00:34:23.870 --> 00:34:26.360
And yes, I would also
like a REST API for that.

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:28.159
And obviously, it
should be secured.

00:34:28.159 --> 00:34:30.710
And you can just take all
those things for granted.

00:34:30.710 --> 00:34:32.810
You literally just
give it a name,

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:38.060
put down the path to the
file, and click Save.

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:39.850
So with that, I'll
pass it over to Edmond

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:44.009
to talk a little bit about
how they implement flexibility

00:34:44.009 --> 00:34:44.530
at Square.

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:51.270
EDMOND CHAN: So 2.75%, it's
what Square is famous for.

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:54.090
It's what we charge for
processing in-person card

00:34:54.090 --> 00:34:55.139
payments.

00:34:55.139 --> 00:34:58.020
But as you can see from the
smaller text below that,

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:00.480
we actually offer custom
rate packages, as well.

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:02.950
And generating those
custom rate packages

00:35:02.950 --> 00:35:07.454
relies on model stacking
and model choice.

00:35:07.454 --> 00:35:09.620
Let's start and walk through
this diagram backwards,

00:35:09.620 --> 00:35:10.280
actually.

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:14.540
Let's begin from the
top right with 2.48%.

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:15.915
That's our custom rate package.

00:35:15.915 --> 00:35:17.290
And what made it?

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:19.370
A Keras model did,
a Keras model that

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:23.270
accepted inputs, that
information that our sales

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:25.250
team has gathered
through their dialogue

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:27.410
with the prospective seller.

00:35:27.410 --> 00:35:30.110
That Keras model itself was
trained on Cloud Machine

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:31.430
Learning engine.

00:35:31.430 --> 00:35:33.650
And here's where the
model stacking happens.

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:36.380
One of the inputs
to this Keras model

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:38.840
is actually a
separate model, a word

00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:40.520
embedding that
we've actually used

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:42.020
through a few other
models, as well.

00:35:45.740 --> 00:35:48.560
One of the other
patterns that we're

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:53.550
going to talk through today
is agility with Yufeng.

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:59.280
YUFENG GUO: So next thing, the
final of our four, is agility.

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:03.810
Lak introduced it with the
example of dinosaurs and birds,

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:05.790
or eagle, in that case.

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:10.470
And the implication here
being that the world changes.

00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:14.670
That's why you're all here
because machine learning has

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:20.640
put down some sweeping changes
on the landscape of computing,

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:22.930
the landscape of technology.

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:26.520
So a couple of models
and mental models

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:30.892
for thinking about how we can
be agile when it comes to data

00:36:30.892 --> 00:36:32.475
and when it comes
to machine learning.

00:36:32.475 --> 00:36:35.340
The first is failing fast.

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:39.150
When you have new
ideas, finding ways

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:45.040
to test out those new
ideas quickly and cheaply.

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:47.650
So if you have a new idea for
an architecture for a house,

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:49.384
maybe you build it
out of ginger house.

00:36:49.384 --> 00:36:50.050
Maybe you don't.

00:36:50.050 --> 00:36:52.030
You use a CAD
software or something.

00:36:52.030 --> 00:36:53.890
But I chose to build
a gingerbread house

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:58.570
and that's what happened
there, so apologies for that.

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:04.260
And when you want to fail
fast, what you can do

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:08.430
is take a subset of your data.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:09.899
This new data client comes in.

00:37:09.899 --> 00:37:11.440
You're like, oh,
let me try this out.

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:14.460
Let me see if I can get
anything useful out of it.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:19.230
But it's not enough to just
randomly sample some new data.

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:24.120
In a lot of cases, you want
to be selective in pulling out

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:27.930
a particular subset of
that data, the part that

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:30.570
is particularly
interesting to you.

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:33.312
Just because you know that
this new data in aggregate

00:37:33.312 --> 00:37:34.770
is something that
is new, something

00:37:34.770 --> 00:37:36.990
that is different from
what you've seen before,

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:40.560
doesn't necessarily mean
that a random sample

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:45.150
will bring about some kind
of new insight for you.

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:47.310
But if you already
know something

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:50.910
about the nature of
this new data, use it.

00:37:50.910 --> 00:37:54.960
Use it to your advantage and
kind of query your data out

00:37:54.960 --> 00:37:58.320
and export a chunk of it that
is representative of what

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:02.640
it is you're trying to evoke.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:06.949
The second aspect of agility is
to basically keep developing.

00:38:06.949 --> 00:38:08.490
Just because a model
is in production

00:38:08.490 --> 00:38:10.320
does not mean you're done.

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:12.030
In fact, you've just started.

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.122
You've just gotten
across the starting line.

00:38:14.122 --> 00:38:15.330
I have a model in production.

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:16.740
Now it's doing stuff.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:21.630
But now we want to continue to
improve that model, especially

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:23.820
with this new live data.

00:38:23.820 --> 00:38:26.280
The example of the dinosaur
data and the bird data

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:28.680
is interesting because
once you start build,

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:32.190
your model's in production and
the new live data is coming in.

00:38:32.190 --> 00:38:35.350
As soon as that
happens, the new data

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:37.860
is now your historical data.

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:44.440
If you can take that new data
and then utilize it to upgrade

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:46.510
or update your models
going forward--

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:48.767
let's say you deploy your
model and two months later,

00:38:48.767 --> 00:38:51.100
you're able to take that two
months worth of data and do

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:53.420
something useful with it--

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:56.890
then you can improve
your existing model.

00:38:56.890 --> 00:38:59.260
You don't need to just
use historical data

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:00.272
from the past past.

00:39:00.272 --> 00:39:01.480
You can have the recent past.

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:08.040
And finally, kind of frequent
retraining, this notion

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:11.100
of once in a while,
you might need

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:14.940
to just blow it
up and start over.

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:18.730
And that's OK because
whether it's a new model--

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:20.580
you might train
it on a new model.

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:22.890
Or you might say,
this model has been

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:25.920
trained on data from
the past five years.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:28.920
But we started by training
the data from the older

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:30.480
three of those five years.

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:32.160
And in the past two
years that the model

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:33.701
has been in production,
we've updated

00:39:33.701 --> 00:39:35.070
and updated and updated.

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:37.320
But there is a lot of cruft.

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:40.410
It's almost like when you're
developing a software system.

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:42.150
You build that initial
release, and then

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:44.490
over the next two
years, you add patches.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:45.720
You put some duct tape here.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:47.800
You add some string over there.

00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:49.690
And at the end of two
years, it doesn't even

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:51.724
look like the thing you
originally designed.

00:39:51.724 --> 00:39:52.890
And so maybe you start over.

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:53.760
You build over here.

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:56.460
And you say, let's
start and say,

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:58.590
if I wanted to build
this from scratch today,

00:39:58.590 --> 00:39:59.890
how would that look like?

00:39:59.890 --> 00:40:01.600
Would I still use
the same models?

00:40:01.600 --> 00:40:04.800
Would I still even
use the same features?

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:07.800
As machine learning, a field
that's moving so quickly, now,

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:11.040
as that kind of
progresses, it will likely

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:14.702
change the answers
to those questions,

00:40:14.702 --> 00:40:15.660
and relatively quickly.

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.970
And so concretely, what
that often amounts to

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:26.470
is taking your deployed
model and having something

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:27.387
like model versioning.

00:40:27.387 --> 00:40:28.720
We all have software versioning.

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:30.094
We have Git and
things like that.

00:40:30.094 --> 00:40:33.190
So we should also do
the same with models.

00:40:33.190 --> 00:40:35.080
And one are the key
benefits of this

00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:37.880
is being able to roll back.

00:40:37.880 --> 00:40:41.040
When you deploy a new
version of a model,

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:44.380
you want to be
able to say, oops.

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:47.110
Actually, no, I
don't want that one.

00:40:47.110 --> 00:40:49.070
Let me go back to the
old one real quick.

00:40:49.070 --> 00:40:51.720
Or maybe you deploy
it and it works.

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:53.715
And then the weekend comes.

00:40:53.715 --> 00:40:55.340
And then you realize
your model doesn't

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:56.870
generalize to the weekend.

00:40:56.870 --> 00:40:59.510
So then you roll back.

00:40:59.510 --> 00:41:02.540
Maybe you have three new
models and different teams

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:05.210
made them and they are
competing for saying,

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:06.707
hey, it should be mine.

00:41:06.707 --> 00:41:09.290
Then the other team also says,
no, no, it should be our model.

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:11.000
Our model is better.

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:15.020
So split testing, being
able to divert your traffic

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:17.510
in an appropriate manner
across different models

00:41:17.510 --> 00:41:21.590
to say which model is actually
the better or more useful

00:41:21.590 --> 00:41:24.410
model.

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:29.030
And the second point here is,
in terms of staying agile,

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:32.720
is thinking of your system
in this kind of chain.

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:36.030
From training all the
way to deployment,

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:38.370
all these different
levels of abstraction

00:41:38.370 --> 00:41:40.470
that you have
access to, you might

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:43.740
start by working with something
like a pre-trained model

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:45.270
or something from
TF Hub, something

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:50.190
that is already well-documented,
well-maintained,

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:53.340
and established as
a model that works.

00:41:53.340 --> 00:41:55.950
And as you go along,
as time passes,

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:59.820
you might become more
sophisticated in your approach

00:41:59.820 --> 00:42:01.860
and customize that model.

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:05.470
And maybe you then retrain
it and do all these things.

00:42:05.470 --> 00:42:08.340
But having good
layers of abstraction

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:10.380
allow you to make these
changes without kind

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:12.560
of colliding with yourself.

00:42:12.560 --> 00:42:14.730
And so to talk a little
more about agility

00:42:14.730 --> 00:42:16.710
and how they've
approached it at Square,

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:17.905
turn it back over to Edmond.

00:42:21.024 --> 00:42:22.690
EDMOND CHAN: So what's
this screen here?

00:42:22.690 --> 00:42:24.272
It's the declined screen.

00:42:24.272 --> 00:42:25.980
It's what our sellers
and their customers

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:28.020
see whenever one of
our financial partners

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:29.830
declines a transaction.

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:31.410
We don't like
seeing this screen.

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:33.840
It represents a lost
sale for our sellers,

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:35.750
and for us it's lost
revenue from missing out

00:42:35.750 --> 00:42:38.640
on facilitating that sale.

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:40.776
In order to minimize
declines, we're

00:42:40.776 --> 00:42:42.150
actually working
on automatically

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:44.010
retrying declines.

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:45.844
But each decline has a cost.

00:42:45.844 --> 00:42:47.760
So clearly, we don't
want to retry everything,

00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:49.830
because some of those
retries will inevitably

00:42:49.830 --> 00:42:51.450
decline themselves.

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:57.080
But we do have a model to help
us decide which ones to retry.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:42:59.590
And this diagram is a lot
simpler than the other ones

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:01.060
I've shown to you.

00:43:01.060 --> 00:43:03.050
And there's really only
two key points here.

00:43:03.050 --> 00:43:06.190
The first is that inference is
done in our payment systems,

00:43:06.190 --> 00:43:07.690
which are written in Java.

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:10.900
The training was done in
Python using a TensorFlow

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:12.250
pre-made classifier.

00:43:12.250 --> 00:43:14.290
And what that has
allowed us to do

00:43:14.290 --> 00:43:16.720
is to go from nothing
to handing off

00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:18.670
a trained model to
the engineering teams

00:43:18.670 --> 00:43:22.480
to integrate into our
payment processing systems.

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:25.850
And while they're working
on integrating that system,

00:43:25.850 --> 00:43:30.430
I'm free to continue to collect
more data to refine our model

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:34.935
and to refine our
decision criteria.

00:43:34.935 --> 00:43:37.310
And finally, when it comes
time to swap models in and out

00:43:37.310 --> 00:43:40.940
for testing, it's
just one line of code

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:42.560
that needs to be
changed, not much.

00:43:54.178 --> 00:43:55.225
YUFENG GUO: Take the mic.

00:43:55.225 --> 00:43:56.350
VALLIAPPA LAKSHMANAN: Sure.

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:56.850
All right.

00:43:56.850 --> 00:44:00.220
So to summarize, then, patterns
to develop, operationalize,

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:02.980
and maintain ML
models, number one,

00:44:02.980 --> 00:44:06.250
need to exactly reproduce
the training steps

00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:07.750
in your deployment system.

00:44:07.750 --> 00:44:11.110
And as Edmond
pointed out, the idea

00:44:11.110 --> 00:44:12.970
is to limit the
training-serving skew

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:14.510
in the machine learning model.

00:44:14.510 --> 00:44:17.050
So use identical
BigQuery queries.

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:19.180
Use TensorFlow feature columns.

00:44:19.180 --> 00:44:21.250
Use TensorFlow transform.

00:44:21.250 --> 00:44:24.430
The second pattern
is to be portable

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:27.040
between different
hardware and software used

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:28.660
for training and inference.

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:31.720
And the point of that
is to basically use

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:34.750
a system, a machine
learning framework, that

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:36.190
offers you that portability.

00:44:36.190 --> 00:44:38.310
Don't go for the next
new, shiny thing.

00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:42.070
There is value in basically
going with something that's

00:44:42.070 --> 00:44:45.760
mature, that's supported on a
variety of different platforms.

00:44:45.760 --> 00:44:47.830
The third aspect of
this is basically

00:44:47.830 --> 00:44:52.240
allowing for flexibility in
model development and model

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:53.930
deployment.

00:44:53.930 --> 00:44:57.670
So the idea there is basically
to be able to change models,

00:44:57.670 --> 00:45:00.700
to be able to basically
stack models together,

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:03.430
to be able to basically
do all of these things,

00:45:03.430 --> 00:45:05.830
to have different
versions of a model.

00:45:05.830 --> 00:45:08.140
All these are very,
very, very important

00:45:08.140 --> 00:45:09.430
in a production system.

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:11.620
And when you design a
machine learning system,

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:14.680
you should plan for your
model to go obsolete,

00:45:14.680 --> 00:45:16.210
for things to be changed.

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:18.580
And make sure that
your client code

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:21.250
doesn't rely on a
very specific model,

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:26.830
but is basically acting on it
through a layer of indirection.

00:45:26.830 --> 00:45:29.200
And finally, this
idea of staying

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:33.340
agile, to be able to iterate
and to deploy updates.

00:45:33.340 --> 00:45:35.650
As Edmond pointed
out, their idea

00:45:35.650 --> 00:45:37.660
was to basically give
their engineering

00:45:37.660 --> 00:45:40.930
team an initial version of
a model and then go ahead

00:45:40.930 --> 00:45:44.500
and keep collecting data to
create the next better model.

00:45:44.500 --> 00:45:46.810
That's a great way
to approach it.

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:49.450
So don't think that you have
to build it all at once.

00:45:49.450 --> 00:45:52.180
But understand that
as new data come in,

00:45:52.180 --> 00:45:54.670
you're going to basically
get a newer and better model.

00:45:54.670 --> 00:45:57.270
But you have to have something
out there immediately.

00:45:57.270 --> 00:46:00.340
So don't let the perfect
be the enemy of the good.

00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:02.980
So to summarize, then,
these are the patterns

00:46:02.980 --> 00:46:06.190
that we talked about for
reproducibility, portability,

00:46:06.190 --> 00:46:07.660
flexibility, and agility.

00:46:07.660 --> 00:46:10.570
Number one, joining
your data in place,

00:46:10.570 --> 00:46:13.480
sharing queries,
sharing data sets,

00:46:13.480 --> 00:46:17.380
preprocessing your data using
Apache Beam and tf.transform,

00:46:17.380 --> 00:46:20.170
running TensorFlow in
hybrid environments,

00:46:20.170 --> 00:46:23.590
thinking about training on the
cloud, predicting on the edge,

00:46:23.590 --> 00:46:27.940
gaining flexibility with
Cloud, with Kubeflow,

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:30.940
building the right abstraction
level in machine learning,

00:46:30.940 --> 00:46:33.550
and building in the
ability in your systems

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:35.950
to be able to roll
model versions

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:38.920
and to continuously retrain
machine learning models.

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:40.060
Thank you.

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:43.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:47.910
[THEME MUSIC]

